to read or write\nL27:   * 【16†Phonics】: the connection between letters and sounds\nL28:   * 【17†Fluency】: reading naturally, while paying attention to meaning\nL29:   * 【18†Comprehension】: understanding and thinking about what you read\nL30:   * 【19†Reading routines】: developing lifelong readers\nL31: \nL32: Was this article helpful?\nL33: [Button: Yes] [Button: No]\nL34: \nL35: 8 out of 8 found this helpful\nL36: \nL37: Have more questions? 【1†Submit a request】\nL38: \nL39: 【20†Return to top】\nL40: ## Comments\nL41: \nL42: 0 comments\nL43: \nL44: Article is closed for comments.\nL45: \nL46: 【21†Khan Academy】\nL47: \nL48: 【22†Powered by Zendesk†www.zendesk.com】\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPhonics: The building blocks of literacy – Khan Academy (https://khankids.zendesk.com/hc/en-us/articles/4403698893965-Phonics-The-building-blocks-of-literacy)\nciteturn1024view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn1023view0\",\"id\":14}); Total lines: 86\nL0: 【0†Skip to main content】\nL1: \nL2: 【1†Submit a request】\nL3: \nL4: [Input][Input] [Input: Search]\nL5: \nL6: Articles in this section\nL7:   * 【2†Our Accessibility Statement】\nL8:   * 【3†Get in touch!】\nL9:   * 【4†Welcome to Khan Academy Kids】\nL10:   * 【5†Parent and Home Account Users: Getting Started and creating an account with Khan Academy Kids】\nL11:   * 【6†How do I set up an account?】\nL12:   * 【7†How do I verify my email?】\nL13:   * 【8†List of resources for parents】\nL14:   * 【9†Printable activities for parents and teachers】\nL15:   * 【10†Weekly Planners for Ages 2-8】\nL16:   * 【11†How does the learning level adjust and how do I view my child's progress?】\nL17: 【12†See more】\nL18: # Phonics: The building blocks of literacy\nL19: \nL20:   * February 26, 2026 23:51\nL21:   * Updated\nL22: ## What is phonics?\nL23: \nL24: Phonics is the connection between letters and the sounds that they make. It can sound intimidating and technical, but phonics is really just the very first step to being able to read on your own.\nL25: \nL26: Phonics instruction covers a lot of different skills and often takes many years to master. It’s typical for a child to begin their phonics journey between the ages of 3 and 5, and keep working on these skills well into 3rd grade. During this process, a child will learn:\nL27:   * The sounds each letter makes\nL28:   * How to identify and say the beginning, middle, and end sounds of words\nL29:   * How to identify and say short and long vowel sounds\nL30:   * The sounds pairs of letters can make together (“sh” or “th” for example)\nL31:   * How to decode words (how to break a word into parts and say each part)\nL32:   * How to blend words (how to put separate sounds together to smoothly read a word)\nL33: ## What can I do to help my child develop their phonics skills?\nL34: \nL35: Lots! The phonics stage of reading acquisition can often feel tedious and boring to kids who just want to get to the fun part of reading. You can help your learner by making phonics practice feel more like a game that’s part of your daily routine. Here are some ideas you can try at home. You can 【13†download】 this tip sheet below.\nL36: \nL37: 【14†Image: KhanKids_Phonics.png】\nL38: ## How does the Khan Kids app help develop phonics skills?\nL39: At 【15†Khan Academy Kids†learn.khanacademy.org】, we love phonics and have a lot of activities that support phonics instruction! Visit the Letters tab in the Library and have your child practice letter sounds with Ollo. Or go to the Reading tab and look for practice on Reading Foundational Skills. This 【16†video of Kodi†youtu.be】 talking about beginning sounds is an example of how your child will be introduced to different elements of phonics such as phonemes (individual sounds) in our app.\nL40: 【17†iframe†www.youtube-nocookie.com】\nL41: \nL42: Because phonics practice can span such a long period of time in your learner’s life, it’s important to follow a sequence to make sure they’re building skills in a helpful order.\nL43: \nL44: Check out the Reading Foundational Skills practice located in the Reading tab of the Library. Here are a few example activities that you can try out in order:\nL45: \nL46:   * Blend Sounds 1\nL47:   * Beginning Sounds 1\nL48:   * Words: End Sounds\nL49: The Khan Kids app has activities for every stage of phonics development, and our short, fun format will keep learners coming back for more!\nL50: ## Additional articles\nL51: \nL52: This is article #2 in our series of 5 articles about the path to reading. If you’d like to learn more, be sure to read the other articles!\nL53: \nL54: Article 1) 【18†Pre-literacy】: the period of time before a child begins to read or write\nL55: \nL56: Article 3) 【19†Fluency】: reading naturally, while paying attention to meaning\nL57: \nL58: Article 4) 【20†Comprehension】: understanding and thinking about what you read\nL59: \nL60: Article 5) 【21†Reading routines】: developing lifelong readers\nL61: ## 👇 Download our phonics tip sheet\nL62: \nL63:   * 【22†What can I do to help my child develop phonics skills?】\nL64: \nL65: 600 KB 【22†Download】\nL66: \nL67: Was this article helpful?\nL68: \nL69: [Button: Yes] [Button: No]\nL70: \nL71: 12 out of 12 found this helpful\nL72: \nL73: Have more questions? 【1†Submit a request】\nL74: \nL75: 【23†Return to top】\nL76: \nL77: ## Comments\nL78: \nL79: 0 comments\nL80: \nL81: Article is closed for comments.\nL82: \nL83: 【24†Khan Academy】\nL84: \nL85: 【25†Powered by Zendesk†www.zendesk.com】\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nComprehension: The ultimate goal of reading – Khan Academy (https://khankids.zendesk.com/hc/en-us/articles/4403706997645-Comprehension-The-ultimate-goal-of-reading)\nciteturn1024view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: text/html; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn1023view0\",\"id\":15}); Total lines: 74\nL0: 【0†Skip to main content】\nL1: \nL2: 【1†Submit a request】\nL3: \nL4: [Input][Input] [Input: Search]\nL5: \nL6: Articles in this section\nL7:   * 【2†Our Accessibility Statement】\nL8:   * 【3†Get in touch!】\nL9:   * 【4†Welcome to Khan Academy Kids】\nL10:   * 【5†Parent and Home Account Users: Getting Started and creating an account with Khan Academy Kids】\nL11:   * 【6†How do I set up an account?】\nL12:   * 【7†How do I verify my email?】\nL13:   * 【8†List of resources for parents】\nL14:   * 【9†Printable activities for parents and teachers】\nL15:   * 【10†Weekly Planners for Ages 2-8】\nL16:   * 【11†How does the learning level adjust and how do I view my child's progress?】\nL17: 【12†See more】\nL18: # Comprehension: The ultimate goal of reading\nL19: \nL20:   * February 26, 2026 23:51\nL21:   * Updated\nL22: ## What is reading comprehension?\nL23: Comprehension is understanding what you read, as you read it. Good readers don’t just say words out loud—they form a picture in their mind of the story, making a mental movie to help them imagine the events as they happen. Readers who are good at comprehending also think beyond the story. They make connections to what they already know, make inferences and predictions, and think about the story after they are done reading.\nL24: Your child has been developing comprehension skills since birth, but now must combine this knowledge with their independent reading abilities.\nL25: ## What can I do to help my child develop comprehension skills?\nL26: \nL27: Continuing to read with your child for fun is a great way to build comprehension skills. When you read together or when you listen to your child read, talk about what you’re reading, new words they might not know, and what you imagine when you read. Here are some more specific tips to help build comprehension at home. You can 【13†download】 this tip sheet below.\nL28: \nL29: 【14†Image: KhanKids_Comprehension.png】\nL30: ## How does the Khan Kids app help to build comprehension?\nL31: \nL32: Reading and thinking about what you read are the best way to build comprehension skills. Luckily, 【15†Khan Academy Kids†learn.khanacademy.org】 has over 300 books built right into our app! You can see all the books in one place by going to the Books tab in the Library. Here, your child can either choose to have the book read aloud to them or to read it on their own.\nL33: Another way to view our books is by clicking on the Reading tab in the Library. Here, books are shown for each age group, and you can view more by clicking on the dropdown menu on the right to change the age you see. When they select a book in the Reading tab, your child will be asked supporting comprehension questions as they read. This can be really helpful as they work on developing their mental movie and comprehension skills.\nL34: Encourage them to pause and answer the question aloud before turning the page.\nL35: Each book in our library is written at an age appropriate level and with vocabulary building in mind. Potentially unfamiliar words are given plenty of context, so that children can learn their meanings. 【16†Here’s a short video†youtu.be】 where Reya explains how to use context clues to learn unfamiliar words. This is a great strategy that your child can use whenever they are reading.\nL36: \nL37: 【17†iframe†www.youtube-nocookie.com】\nL38: ## Additional articles\nL39: \nL40: This is article #4 in our series of 5 articles about the path to reading. If you’d like to learn more, be sure to read the other articles!\nL41: \nL42: Article 1) 【18†Pre-literacy】: the period of time before a child begins to read or write\nL43: \nL44: Article 2) 【19†Phonics】: the connection between letters and sounds\nL45: \nL46: Article 3) 【20†Fluency】: reading naturally, while paying attention to meaning\nL47: \nL48: Article 5) 【21†Reading routines】: developing lifelong readers\nL49: ## 👇 Download our reading comprehension tip sheet\nL50: \nL51:   * 【22†KhanKids_Comprehension.pdf】\nL52: \nL53: 700 KB 【22†Download】\nL54: \nL55: 